# Zod Validation Best Practices

## Schema Definition

- Define schemas close to where they're used or in dedicated schema files
- Use descriptive schema names (e.g., `loginSchema`, `userProfileSchema`)
- Export schemas for reuse across client and server
- Use `.strict()` to prevent unexpected fields
- Implement proper error messages with `.refine()` and custom error maps
- Use branded types for additional type safety

## Form Validation

- Validate on client-side before submission
- Always validate again on server-side
- Use `.safeParse()` for validation without throwing
- Parse form data with `z.object()` schemas
- Handle validation errors in UI appropriately
- Show field-specific error messages

## API Validation

- Validate all incoming request data
- Use Zod schemas for request body, params, and query validation
- Return proper error responses for validation failures
- Type API responses with Zod schemas
- Use `.transform()` for data normalization
- Implement proper error handling

## Complex Schemas

- Use `.refine()` for custom validation logic
- Implement cross-field validation with `.superRefine()`
- Use discriminated unions for polymorphic data
- Leverage `.extend()` to build upon existing schemas
- Use `.pick()` and `.omit()` for schema variations
- Implement conditional validation with `.refine()`

## TypeScript Integration

- Infer TypeScript types from Zod schemas with `z.infer<>`
- Use Zod schemas as single source of truth for types
- Export both schema and inferred type together
- Use Zod for runtime validation and compile-time types
- Avoid duplicating types and schemas
- Use `.brand()` for nominal typing

## Performance

- Cache parsed schemas when possible
- Use `.partial()` for optional updates
- Implement lazy validation for large forms
- Use `.passthrough()` sparingly
- Avoid overly complex nested schemas
- Parse only what you need

## Error Handling

- Use custom error maps for user-friendly messages
- Format Zod errors for display in UI
- Log validation failures appropriately
- Return proper HTTP status codes for API validation
- Provide specific field-level errors
- Handle edge cases gracefully

## Common Patterns

- Email validation: `z.string().email()`
- Password strength: Custom refinement with clear rules
- Date validation: `z.coerce.date()` or `z.string().datetime()`
- Optional fields: `.optional()` or `.nullable()`
- Default values: `.default()`
- Enums: `z.enum()` or `z.nativeEnum()`
